<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

* ''HIGH — `tools/launch-codex.mjs:273,278`'': The watchdog identifies the seat only by numeric PID. If that process exits and the PID is reused before the next poll, `alive()` can mistake the replacement for the seat and signal its process group. The detached process-group kill is otherwise appropriate for the child launched with `detached: true`, but it needs an identity check to protect against PID reuse.

* ''MED — `tools/launch-codex.mjs:81,312`'': Rollout matching can fail or select the wrong seat for valid paths containing commas or JSON-escaped characters. The launcher comma-joins cwd values, then the watchdog splits them; matching compares those raw strings to the regex-captured, still-escaped `cwd`. With concurrent seats sharing a worktree, choosing the newest matching rollout can also make both watchdogs follow the same session.

* ''MED — `tools/launch-codex.test.mjs:389–401`'': The matching test covers distinct plain cwd values and an old mtime, but not escaped/comma paths or simultaneous seats sharing a cwd. It therefore does not catch the matching defects above.

* ''LOW — `tools/launch-codex.test.mjs:403–430`'': Watchdog behavior tests exercise injected callbacks, not the real PID/process-group signaling or rollout polling path. They verify the loop’s logic, but would still pass if `watchMain` targeted the wrong process group. The pool refusal tests do verify threshold refusals and boundary controls; I found no refusal-ordering defect.

<<~/ahu>>

